# Set font (affects titlebars and waybar if using Swaybar)
font pango:JetBrains Mono Nerd Font SemiBold 11

# Disable default swaybg
swaybg_command echo

# Waybar (hidden by default, toggle with mod+w)
# swaybar_command waybar

# Remove window titlebars
default_floating_border none
hide_edge_borders --i3 smart
# `focus`, not `smart`, on purpose. With `smart`, sway focuses an activating window only if
# it is *already visible* — otherwise it merely flags it urgent (man 5 sway). Vivaldi's main
# window and its PWAs are one process (one Wayland connection, one Chromium focus state
# machine) split across two workspaces by the assign rules in ./rules. So opening a link from
# the Gmail PWA on ws10 into the main window on ws2 made Vivaldi activate a window sway was
# still keeping unfocused and off-screen. Vivaldi then drove its UI against that surface, and
# anything grab-like it opened (menu/bubble popups are child surfaces, not sway containers —
# they never show up in the tree) left the window modally blocked: it behaved exactly like an
# unclosed popup with no popup anywhere to find.
#
# `focus` makes sway actually follow Vivaldi to ws2, so the window is visible and focused
# before Vivaldi draws on it. Tradeoff: any client can now pull focus across workspaces.
focus_on_window_activation focus
force_display_urgency_hint 100

### Mouse
focus_follows_mouse no

### Window Gaps
gaps inner 8

# Borders
default_border pixel 2
smart_borders on
